# Emberline -- the rules of a real-time hold.
#
# Three keepers stand on the near ranks of a burning line and hold it against things that come
# out of the dark. Nothing in this game takes a turn: the host advances a tick clock from its own
# fixed timestep, twenty ticks a second, and every length in this content is written in seconds.
#
#   dotnet cantrip lint realtime/content --warnings-as-errors
#   dotnet cantrip test realtime/content

# `clock ticks` is what makes `cooldown 4s` and `on every 2s:` checkable rather than hopeful: a
# `2 turns` anywhere below is error CT325 at lint, and a `move`, a `pattern`, a `stacking duration`
# or an `until turn_end:` is error CT337, instead of a line that quietly never fires.
# `turns:` and `order:` are deliberately absent -- they are CT335 under this clock.
#
# `ends: called` is what makes a hold possible at all. By default a battle is won the moment the
# last enemy is gone, and in a wave game the board is empty every few seconds by design, so the
# first run of this ended at two seconds, having been won. Here the game says when the fight is
# over: the host calls `EndBattle` when the forty-five seconds are up, or the party falls.
ruleset
  clock ticks
  ends: called
  max_hand_size 10

# Three lanes wide, five ranks deep, mirrored. The keepers hold rank 0; everything hostile walks
# in at rank 3 and closes one rank at a time, so the distance between a keeper and a hollow is
# 1 when it is on top of them and 4 when it has just arrived. Every ability's `range` is written
# against those numbers, and that is the whole of the game's tension: what you can reach. The
# fifth rank is standing room: a wave lands on the first free slot of lane 0 and walks to its own
# lane from there, so the board needs somewhere to put one that arrives in a crowd.
board "The Line"
  lanes 3
  ranks 5
  facing
  metric manhattan
  on_vacated gap
  lane_word "lane"
  rank_word "rank"

hero "Warden"
  hp 34
  abilities "Bulwark", "Haul"

hero "Lantern"
  hp 26
  abilities "Signal Flare", "Mend"
